From: Sam Price <thesamprice@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 13:29:02 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 0/2] microblaze: __moddi3 is wrong for almost every input

libgcc's MicroBlaze __moddi3 has been wrong since the port landed in 2010.
It returns the wrong answer for most inputs on big-endian, different wrong
answers on little-endian, and does not terminate at all for LLONG_MIN on
either.

These are two ways to fix it, and you should apply one or the other, not
both.  I measured them rather than guessing, and would rather ask than choose
for you.

  1/2  Ramin Moussavi's fix to the existing assembly, with my additions.
  2/2  Delete the assembly and use the generic libgcc2.c version.

Each patch applies to an unpatched master on its own; 2/2 is not a follow-on
to 1/2.  Both touch libgcc only, and there are no compiler changes.

Which test proves it
--------------------

gcc.c-torture/execute/arith-rand-ll.c.  It calls __moddi3 through its

    { signed long long xx = x, yy = y, r1, r2; ... r2 = xx % yy; ... }

block over 10000 random bitstrings and checks r1 * yy + r2 == xx, so a wrong
remainder fails the invariant and it calls abort().  Run on
qemu-system-microblaze and qemu-system-microblazeel, machine
petalogix-s3adsp1800:

                      unpatched    with 1/2    with 2/2
  microblaze-elf        abort        pass        pass
  microblazeel-elf      abort        pass        pass

So the bug is provable and each fix is confirmed with a test already in the
tree, on both byte orders, and no new test is required.  More on that below.

2/2 is tempting because MicroBlaze is the only target in the tree with a
hand-written assembly moddi3 -- visium has a C one, bpf excludes the helper,
everyone else uses the generic version.  A routine no other port felt the need
to hand-write, and which turned out to be wrong for sixteen years, is a
reasonable thing to delete.

The measurements say otherwise.  qemu-system-microblaze and
qemu-system-microblazeel, machine petalogix-s3adsp1800, 4701 vectors with
expected values computed on the host, executed instructions counted exactly
with -icount 0.  The generic __moddi3 was compiled at -O2, the level libgcc
builds LIB2FUNCS at:

                       code size      executed insns
  assembly __moddi3        55      be 3605340  le 3605277
  generic C at -O2         62      be 3995016  le 3988505
    + __udivmoddi4     be 1030
                       le 1028
    total              be 1092       +10.8%      +10.6%
                       le 1090

The assembly is about 20 times smaller and about 10% faster -- roughly 82
instructions per call.  On an embedded target that is not a close call, so my
recommendation is 1/2.  One caveat in fairness to 2/2: __udivmoddi4 is shared,
so a program already doing 64-bit unsigned division links it anyway, and the
generic __moddi3 then costs 62 instructions rather than 1092.

Division by zero
----------------

Undefined behaviour either way, but the two disagree and it is worth stating.

  assembly            x % 0  ->  0
  generic C at -O2    x % 0  ->  low word of x   (0x123456789abc -> 0x9abc)

The C result is not the generic code misbehaving so much as an assumption that
does not hold here.  libgcc2.c does "d0 = 1 / d0" to provoke a hardware
divide-by-zero trap, but on MicroBlaze that compiles to a call to __udivsi3,
whose own divide-by-zero path returns zero, so no trap occurs and the
algorithm continues with d0 still zero.

Zero is the more defensible answer, since it is what divsi3.S, modsi3.S,
udivsi3.S and umodsi3.S all return, and 1/2 records that in a comment.

Worth noting separately, since it predates this series and is not something
either patch changes: neither matches a core with hardware divide.  MicroBlaze
has no remainder instruction, so GCC implements a % b as a - (a / b) * b, and
idiv by zero yields a zero quotient, so a % 0 evaluates to a.  Measured under
QEMU, 5 % 0 gives 5 with hardware divide against 0 through the helper.  The
hardware also sets MSR[DZ] and raises a divide exception where the core
implements one and MSR[EE] is set, which Linux reports as SIGFPE/FPE_INTDIV;
a software helper can do none of that.  Aligning the two would touch all five
helpers and belongs in its own discussion.

No new test, and why
--------------------

As above, arith-rand-ll.c already covers this.  I checked, having first assumed
otherwise -- its first division block is unsigned, and it was only on reading
further that the signed long long block turned up.

Which raises the more useful point.  The coverage exists and works, so the
reason a routine this broken went unnoticed from 2010 to 2026 is that the
execute testsuite does not appear to be run for MicroBlaze at all.  Fixing
__moddi3 does not fix that, and it is likely not the only thing hiding.

Coverage of the routine 1/2 produces, traced with a QEMU TCG plugin over the
4701 vectors: 55 of 55 instructions and 17 of 18 conditional branch outcomes,
identically on both byte orders.  The single missing outcome is the branch to
the divide-by-zero label, which the vectors deliberately exclude.

Provenance, and what I changed
------------------------------

The assembly fix in 1/2 is Ramin Moussavi's, from OpenADK.  He has agreed to
my submitting it, and 1/2 credits him in its first paragraph; I am signing it
off because the additions below are mine and he is not sending it himself.

I changed five things.  Two of them reach the instruction stream, so here is
what they cost, measured the same way as above:

                        code size      executed insns
  his patch as sent          56      be 3607673  le 3607610
  1/2 as sent here           55      be 3605340  le 3605277

That is one instruction less of code and 2333 fewer executed instructions
over the 4701 calls, the same on both byte orders.  The two changes:

  - Removed $LaResult_Is_Zero, and with it the BRI that existed only to jump
    over the label.  His change redirects the only branch that reached
    $LaResult_Is_Zero, so it was left unreferenced, and the sign fix-up can
    now fall through into the empty divide-by-zero label instead of
    branching past it.

  - Restored the delay slot on the first zero test.  His version turns
    BEQID into BEQI, leaving the following OR -- which computes the divisor
    zero test and is dead at the branch target -- outside the slot.  Putting
    it back in the slot is free.

The other three do not:

  - Added the divide-by-zero comment described above.

  - Tidied the surrounding comments, and annotated the loop body,
    normalization and sign fix-up against the reference algorithm.  The
    header still said "modsi3.S ... modulo operation for 64 bit integers";
    several comments transcribed the instruction instead of explaining it;
    one described the dividend by register number after the halves were
    renamed; and the epilogue claimed to restore CSRs and the operands when
    it restores callee-saved registers.  Trailing whitespace on the touched
    lines is gone.  Stripping every comment from the result leaves .text
    byte-identical on both byte orders, so none of this reached the code.

  - Corrected the commit message.  It said big-endian got 30079 of 40420
    values wrong while little-endian hung, which reads as though big-endian
    completes.  It does not: both byte orders hang, at the same point, after
    the same five wrong answers.  His own text says as much further down
    ("LLONG_MIN % 1 hangs on both endiannesses"), so the summary contradicted
    the detail.

Algorithm, for anyone comparing the two
---------------------------------------

They are different algorithms, which is why the measurements differ by tens
of percent rather than a constant factor.

  1/2  restoring division, radix 2, one quotient bit per iteration.  Koren,
       Computer Arithmetic Algorithms, section 3.2, eq. 3.11.  The restore
       is implicit: the trial subtraction goes to scratch and is kept only
       when non-negative, which is the variant Koren notes avoids the
       restore penalty.  The file now says so in its header, and the loop,
       the normalization step and the sign fix-up each name the part of that
       section they implement.

  2/2  Knuth's Algorithm D, TAOCP volume 2 section 4.3.1 -- digit
       recurrence at radix 2^16 with quotient-digit estimation and
       add-back.  __udiv_qrnnd_c in longlong.h is step D3 and its
       correction; UDIV_NEEDS_NORMALIZATION is D1.

Koren is also where the rule defect 3 broke is stated outright: the sign of
the remainder is the sign of the dividend, not the exclusive or of the
operand signs.  That exclusive or is the quotient's rule, and it is what the
original code used.

